<crash-analysis>
The crash is caused by a bug in the project code, specifically in the `ini_parse_stream` function in `ini.c`.

Analysis:
The crash occurs at `ini.c:168` inside a `while` loop that reads lines from the input stream. The code reads a line into the `abyss` buffer using `fgets`. If `fgets` reads an empty line (which can happen with certain inputs), the `abyss` buffer will contain an empty string. In this case, `strlen(abyss)` returns 0. The code then attempts to access `abyss[strlen(abyss) - 1]`, which becomes `abyss[0 - 1]`, or `abyss[-1]`. This is an out-of-bounds read, causing a stack-buffer-overflow and crashing the program.

Suggestion:
To fix this bug, a check should be added before accessing `abyss` to ensure that `strlen(abyss)` is greater than 0.

```c
// inih/ini.c:168
// Before:
if (abyss[strlen(abyss) - 1] == '\n')

// After:
if (strlen(abyss) > 0 && abyss[strlen(abyss) - 1] == '\n')
```
This change will prevent the out-of-bounds read when an empty line is encountered.
</crash-analysis>

